00:01:31.670 --> 00:01:32.910
What is grid computing?

00:01:32.910 --> 00:01:34.980
I guess, to me--

00:01:34.980 --> 00:01:37.890
not to read the words
exactly, but basically

00:01:37.890 --> 00:01:40.920
having a large cluster with
a set of nodes that we're

00:01:40.920 --> 00:01:43.090
going to do some kind of
analytic calculation with,

00:01:43.090 --> 00:01:44.756
is generally the way
that I approach it.

00:01:44.756 --> 00:01:47.000
Now that could be
deterministic stuff

00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:49.800
that I've coded in myself,
could be elements of ML as well.

00:01:49.800 --> 00:01:52.890
But basically, I look
at it as a large cluster

00:01:52.890 --> 00:01:56.740
of calculation agents that
are doing some set of tasks.

00:01:56.740 --> 00:02:00.030
Generally, hopefully you can
make them relatively stateless

00:02:00.030 --> 00:02:02.130
and do it in a relatively
independent way,

00:02:02.130 --> 00:02:04.170
so when you aggregate
and bring the data back,

00:02:04.170 --> 00:02:07.380
that could be thought
up and thought out

00:02:07.380 --> 00:02:11.070
in a relatively
straightforward manner.

00:02:11.070 --> 00:02:13.480
So as with most
things in my life,

00:02:13.480 --> 00:02:15.440
I started with three wishes.

00:02:15.440 --> 00:02:17.415
It's not asking for
infinitely many wishes.

00:02:17.415 --> 00:02:18.750
It's not what a wish is.

00:02:18.750 --> 00:02:21.250
Or a large but finite amount
of wishes is one of the wishes,

00:02:21.250 --> 00:02:22.410
I guess.

00:02:22.410 --> 00:02:24.655
So when I approached
and talked to Reza

00:02:24.655 --> 00:02:28.710
and we were discussing
this, I guess in London,

00:02:28.710 --> 00:02:31.531
it was actually in a pub when we
first started talking about it.

00:02:31.531 --> 00:02:33.780
REZA ROKNI: As all good
engineering projects in London

00:02:33.780 --> 00:02:34.045
are.

00:02:34.045 --> 00:02:35.850
NEIL BOSTON: As all good
engineering projects in London

00:02:35.850 --> 00:02:37.349
are, near a place
called Farringdon,

00:02:37.349 --> 00:02:39.780
which was very good,
I started writing down

00:02:39.780 --> 00:02:41.520
what my users'
stories were, but they

00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:43.690
became three wishes basically.

00:02:43.690 --> 00:02:46.050
So I wanted to write single
threaded simple calculation

00:02:46.050 --> 00:02:48.325
class as functors or packages.

00:02:48.325 --> 00:02:50.700
I didn't have to worry about
things like multi-threading,

00:02:50.700 --> 00:02:52.200
for example.

00:02:52.200 --> 00:02:55.020
I wanted to stop thinking
about creating my own

00:02:55.020 --> 00:02:58.410
directed acyclic graphs,
bitemporal or not.

00:02:58.410 --> 00:03:01.140
I just basically wanted to
focus on my IP and my analytics

00:03:01.140 --> 00:03:02.345
in a very simple way.

00:03:02.345 --> 00:03:04.860
But I like to be able
to tweak my experiments

00:03:04.860 --> 00:03:07.650
and run my experiments in
a much more seamless way,

00:03:07.650 --> 00:03:09.840
rather than having to
recompile and rebuild

00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:11.434
a lot of my analytic classes.

00:03:11.434 --> 00:03:13.350
So I wanted some environment
I could basically

00:03:13.350 --> 00:03:17.200
drop my functors inside,
run a set of experiments,

00:03:17.200 --> 00:03:20.130
get set of results out,
and do that repeatedly,

00:03:20.130 --> 00:03:25.560
either manually or through a
set of machines, potentially.

00:03:25.560 --> 00:03:28.630
So three simple wishes, I guess.

00:03:28.630 --> 00:03:30.060
Challenges.

00:03:30.060 --> 00:03:35.520
I guess, I've been in markets
technology for 150 years now,

00:03:35.520 --> 00:03:37.260
so a long time.

00:03:37.260 --> 00:03:40.360
So quants, I wanted
to really focus

00:03:40.360 --> 00:03:43.030
on my quantitative analysis,
people writing maths.

00:03:43.030 --> 00:03:46.080
I just wanted to focus on
the maths and the outcomes

00:03:46.080 --> 00:03:47.990
rather than thinking
about this whole fabric.

00:03:47.990 --> 00:03:51.430
So over the years I've
been in markets technology,

00:03:51.430 --> 00:03:54.720
you can spend 20% of your time
thinking about the mathematics

00:03:54.720 --> 00:03:57.990
and 80% of your time thinking
about how you make this thing

00:03:57.990 --> 00:04:00.840
scalable, how you make
it distributed, how

00:04:00.840 --> 00:04:02.297
this thing can scale.

00:04:02.297 --> 00:04:04.380
And actually, one of the
challenges as well is you

00:04:04.380 --> 00:04:05.680
can spend a lot of your time--

00:04:05.680 --> 00:04:07.740
I guess my background is C++--

00:04:07.740 --> 00:04:12.120
is trying to optimize your
C++ code to run on your PC,

00:04:12.120 --> 00:04:15.070
which is obviously
diminishing returns of scale.

00:04:15.070 --> 00:04:17.399
So again, I wanted to try
and get away from that.

00:04:17.399 --> 00:04:19.589
I wanted the people to
focus on the mathematicians

00:04:19.589 --> 00:04:21.209
and physicists and
engineers, to focus

00:04:21.209 --> 00:04:24.660
on running experiments,
rather than thinking

00:04:24.660 --> 00:04:29.040
about the other aspects about
the ecosystem, and much more

00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:31.860
about the maths, the data
coming back and the outcomes,

00:04:31.860 --> 00:04:33.510
and analyzing that,
either manually

00:04:33.510 --> 00:04:35.760
or, again, through machines.

00:04:35.760 --> 00:04:38.410
And I wanted to be able
to do it very, very fast.

00:04:38.410 --> 00:04:40.170
So if I didn't know
which model to choose

00:04:40.170 --> 00:04:42.090
and I had a category
of models, to be

00:04:42.090 --> 00:04:44.940
able to get to some reasonable
outcome in a relatively

00:04:44.940 --> 00:04:47.700
quick way by running
experiments at high velocity

00:04:47.700 --> 00:04:49.850
was really the challenge.

00:04:49.850 --> 00:04:55.410
So kind of utopia, I guess,
from a of quant prospective.

00:04:55.410 --> 00:05:00.270
Opportunities, running it on
demand and also event driven.

00:05:00.270 --> 00:05:03.570
There are two sides very
much in markets technology.

00:05:03.570 --> 00:05:06.710
I want to sometimes run things
at very large scale and lots

00:05:06.710 --> 00:05:09.187
of things, which you'll see
later in the experiment.

00:05:09.187 --> 00:05:11.270
But I also have things
coming in at high frequency

00:05:11.270 --> 00:05:13.187
that I want to
deal with as well.

00:05:13.187 --> 00:05:15.270
So you can think of as a
bifurcation a little bit,

00:05:15.270 --> 00:05:20.222
but you don't want a bifurcation
in ecosystem or fabric.

00:05:20.222 --> 00:05:21.680
I want to be able
to run a universe

00:05:21.680 --> 00:05:25.670
of parallel experiments and
then do some assessment of them,

00:05:25.670 --> 00:05:27.950
either through some
parametrization,

00:05:27.950 --> 00:05:30.290
through some MLPs,
or again, manually,

00:05:30.290 --> 00:05:33.085
just looking at these
things in a reasonable way,

00:05:33.085 --> 00:05:36.590
and then run huge, I
guess, simulations.

00:05:36.590 --> 00:05:39.140
So if you come from banking
and have a look at the way

00:05:39.140 --> 00:05:42.230
the market's gone,
especially with regulation

00:05:42.230 --> 00:05:45.710
in recent years, running
large Monte Carlo simulations,

00:05:45.710 --> 00:05:48.941
for example, [? onport ?]
entire portfolios across all

00:05:48.941 --> 00:05:51.440
the markets that the investment
bank of the markets division

00:05:51.440 --> 00:05:55.107
has is, again, a
significant challenge.

00:05:55.107 --> 00:05:56.690
I'll talk about it
a little bit later,

00:05:56.690 --> 00:05:58.490
about the organizational
issues that you

00:05:58.490 --> 00:06:00.050
have around some
of the technology

00:06:00.050 --> 00:06:02.150
choices the banks make
to try and accommodate

00:06:02.150 --> 00:06:03.690
some of those solutions.

00:06:03.690 --> 00:06:06.720
But again, regardless of
the simulation experiment,

00:06:06.720 --> 00:06:10.667
I wanted a relatively stable
and consistent ETL style

00:06:10.667 --> 00:06:12.500
environment that I
wasn't too worried about.

00:06:15.010 --> 00:06:18.542
Now I'll hand over to Reza, and
he can talk through Dataflow.

00:06:18.542 --> 00:06:19.250
REZA ROKNI: Sure.

00:06:19.250 --> 00:06:22.640
And so this is interesting,
because Dataflow

00:06:22.640 --> 00:06:25.440
we use predominantly in
things like ETL pipelines.

00:06:25.440 --> 00:06:28.040
So we're taking data,
we're processing it,

00:06:28.040 --> 00:06:30.860
we are passing that on
to things like BigQuery.

00:06:30.860 --> 00:06:34.232
So why are we using
this for grid computing?

00:06:34.232 --> 00:06:36.732
To look at this-- and this was
after many conversations when

00:06:36.732 --> 00:06:38.990
we were sitting down and
storyboarding this stuff

00:06:38.990 --> 00:06:40.860
and looking at architectures--

00:06:40.860 --> 00:06:43.370
you kind of have to
deconstruct what a grid is,

00:06:43.370 --> 00:06:45.360
and then it makes
perfect sense why

00:06:45.360 --> 00:06:48.480
Dataflow is a very good tool
for solving this problem.

00:06:48.480 --> 00:06:49.250
So what is a grid?

00:06:49.250 --> 00:06:51.410
First of all, it's data.

00:06:51.410 --> 00:06:53.570
It's a bunch of math
functions, so smart maths

00:06:53.570 --> 00:06:55.580
folks like these guys.

00:06:55.580 --> 00:06:57.530
You need lots of
CPUs, and you need

00:06:57.530 --> 00:06:59.420
some way of scheduling
jobs on those CPUs.

00:06:59.420 --> 00:07:02.610
So you need to get data
and functions to the CPUs

00:07:02.610 --> 00:07:05.641
and get them to do the
work, and you need storage.

00:07:05.641 --> 00:07:07.890
So let's go through those
in a little bit more detail.

00:07:07.890 --> 00:07:10.550
And we start getting
into why Dataflow

00:07:10.550 --> 00:07:13.290
is particularly useful here.

00:07:13.290 --> 00:07:15.884
So in terms of data, there's
quite a few data sources.

00:07:15.884 --> 00:07:18.050
First, there's internal
data sources, so the trades,

00:07:18.050 --> 00:07:20.230
et cetera, that are
happening within the bank.

00:07:20.230 --> 00:07:21.710
There's a lot of
information there.

00:07:21.710 --> 00:07:23.690
There's external data
sources potentially

00:07:23.690 --> 00:07:27.530
from market environments, all
of these raw underlying data

00:07:27.530 --> 00:07:29.630
that we need to then
do some processing.

00:07:29.630 --> 00:07:31.520
And then there's derived data.

00:07:31.520 --> 00:07:34.250
So this is data where you've
taken underlying information,

00:07:34.250 --> 00:07:35.746
you've done some
processing on it,

00:07:35.746 --> 00:07:38.120
and you've got some new data
that you need to distribute,

00:07:38.120 --> 00:07:41.300
for example, yield curves
that are iterating based off

00:07:41.300 --> 00:07:43.430
of things like swaps
and deposit information

00:07:43.430 --> 00:07:44.810
that you have early on.

00:07:44.810 --> 00:07:47.570
Now the next piece
around data is

00:07:47.570 --> 00:07:49.870
that it doesn't come
in a nice, static file.

00:07:49.870 --> 00:07:50.900
It's streamed.

00:07:50.900 --> 00:07:53.060
It's continuously being updated.

00:07:53.060 --> 00:07:55.250
Again, this is starting
to get interesting

00:07:55.250 --> 00:07:57.500
in terms of what Dataflow
is capable of, which

00:07:57.500 --> 00:07:59.940
is being able to do stream
processing on that datasets

00:07:59.940 --> 00:08:01.770
that are coming in.

00:08:01.770 --> 00:08:03.650
Next, it's functions.

00:08:03.650 --> 00:08:04.670
It's maths functions.

00:08:04.670 --> 00:08:06.860
It's a lot of CPUs.

00:08:06.860 --> 00:08:09.170
One of the interesting
challenges, fun bits,

00:08:09.170 --> 00:08:12.380
we had is a lot of the quant
code that's been written

00:08:12.380 --> 00:08:17.240
in the last 20 years,
I guess, is C++.

00:08:17.240 --> 00:08:22.340
And Dataflow today does not
support C++ as a standard

00:08:22.340 --> 00:08:23.520
language.

00:08:23.520 --> 00:08:26.510
And we had to do some work,
and I worked with Reuven to get

00:08:26.510 --> 00:08:29.490
Dataflow flow to
run C++ code for us.

00:08:29.490 --> 00:08:31.059
So that was a little bit of fun.

00:08:31.059 --> 00:08:32.600
NEIL BOSTON: So I
was just going say,

00:08:32.600 --> 00:08:36.679
I guess the 3.1 wish is I
didn't want to JNI into the C++.

00:08:36.679 --> 00:08:38.750
I wanted to think of it
in a much more, I guess,

00:08:38.750 --> 00:08:40.066
natural way.

00:08:40.066 --> 00:08:42.440
So I guess that was my 3.1
wishes, rather than just three

00:08:42.440 --> 00:08:43.309
wishes.

00:08:43.309 --> 00:08:45.680
REZA ROKNI: And I found out
from Reuven that actually,

00:08:45.680 --> 00:08:49.762
C++ is the original
Flume, right?

00:08:49.762 --> 00:08:51.470
REUVEN LAX: Yes, so
internally at Google,

00:08:51.470 --> 00:08:54.130
we do run a version
of Dataflow on C++.

00:08:54.130 --> 00:08:57.200
There was just-- at the time
we came out with Dataflow,

00:08:57.200 --> 00:09:00.380
there wasn't as much of a
cloud market interested in C++.

00:09:00.380 --> 00:09:02.660
Most people were interested
in Java or Python

00:09:02.660 --> 00:09:05.160
or other languages.

00:09:05.160 --> 00:09:06.680
REZA ROKNI: So we
basically made--

00:09:06.680 --> 00:09:08.510
retrofitted C++
running on Dataflow,

00:09:08.510 --> 00:09:12.740
even though the original
FlumeJava was all C++.

00:09:12.740 --> 00:09:15.810
The other piece around this
is kind of that ETL work.

00:09:15.810 --> 00:09:17.780
So you're having to munge data.

00:09:17.780 --> 00:09:20.630
You have to shape it, enrich it.

00:09:20.630 --> 00:09:22.620
And then once you've
got that data,

00:09:22.620 --> 00:09:24.170
there's a place of storing it.

00:09:24.170 --> 00:09:25.550
You need to then store--

00:09:25.550 --> 00:09:28.700
pull from storage within
the pipeline itself.

00:09:28.700 --> 00:09:32.070
You may potentially need to
do output to storage as well.

00:09:32.070 --> 00:09:33.950
And these grids
have huge fan-out.

00:09:33.950 --> 00:09:36.800
So I can take a couple of megs
worth of market information,

00:09:36.800 --> 00:09:38.570
or even, let's
say, a gig, and it

00:09:38.570 --> 00:09:40.749
generates terabytes of output.

00:09:40.749 --> 00:09:42.290
So we need some way
of actually being

00:09:42.290 --> 00:09:46.220
able to deal with huge fan-out
from these environments.

00:09:46.220 --> 00:09:47.960
And then there's one
secret ingredient

00:09:47.960 --> 00:09:50.840
that actually means that
one of-- the testing we've

00:09:50.840 --> 00:09:53.930
done so far has been
very, very productive.

00:09:53.930 --> 00:09:57.330
And that secret ingredient
is the movement of data.

00:09:57.330 --> 00:10:01.130
So if you think about the
fact that we're pulling source

00:10:01.130 --> 00:10:03.260
information, we're doing ETL.

00:10:03.260 --> 00:10:05.760
Then we're sending that data
to the next stage, which

00:10:05.760 --> 00:10:08.065
is doing the processing
with that data,

00:10:08.065 --> 00:10:09.830
so running those
maths functions.

00:10:09.830 --> 00:10:12.600
And then we're sending that
data again to the storage.

00:10:12.600 --> 00:10:15.150
These are all chunks
of data movement.

00:10:15.150 --> 00:10:16.950
Now with Dataflow,
we can actually

00:10:16.950 --> 00:10:19.670
create a DAG, a distributed--

00:10:19.670 --> 00:10:21.630
a Directed Acyclic
Graph, sorry--

00:10:21.630 --> 00:10:22.590
of computation.

00:10:22.590 --> 00:10:26.190
And those connections between
all of the nodes, Dataflow

00:10:26.190 --> 00:10:29.496
takes care of moving
the elements for me.

00:10:29.496 --> 00:10:30.870
So this is, again,
why we started

00:10:30.870 --> 00:10:32.820
thinking, actually,
Dataflow is a very

00:10:32.820 --> 00:10:34.840
good fit for this environment.

00:10:34.840 --> 00:10:37.869
And when we think about
this movement of data,

00:10:37.869 --> 00:10:40.410
one thing I noticed, because I
don't actually originally come

00:10:40.410 --> 00:10:42.990
from a finance
background, was you

00:10:42.990 --> 00:10:46.180
see a lot of organizational
structure coming

00:10:46.180 --> 00:10:48.790
into how the DAG is created.

00:10:48.790 --> 00:10:51.390
So by that, I mean there's a
team that does some ETL work,

00:10:51.390 --> 00:10:52.930
and they generate a file.

00:10:52.930 --> 00:10:55.860
This file is then moved to the
team that does the grid work.

00:10:55.860 --> 00:10:58.170
And then the grid work
outputs another file,

00:10:58.170 --> 00:11:01.050
which is moved to the team
that does the analytics work.

00:11:01.050 --> 00:11:03.420
And what that is
doing is essentially

00:11:03.420 --> 00:11:05.130
creating this
organizational structure

00:11:05.130 --> 00:11:06.990
on top of how the
technology should look,

00:11:06.990 --> 00:11:08.490
but that's not
really what you want.

00:11:08.490 --> 00:11:10.920
What you want is one
continuous operation

00:11:10.920 --> 00:11:11.970
from beginning to end.

00:11:11.970 --> 00:11:16.040
And Neil will explain this in
better detail than I could.

00:11:16.040 --> 00:11:18.690
NEIL BOSTON: So I
guess this slide here,

00:11:18.690 --> 00:11:21.720
this schematic, it's not
specific to the banking

00:11:21.720 --> 00:11:22.760
industry.

00:11:22.760 --> 00:11:24.357
But the way that
I've perceived it,

00:11:24.357 --> 00:11:26.190
over many industries
over the years, is they

00:11:26.190 --> 00:11:29.460
tend to design technology
by organizational construct.

00:11:29.460 --> 00:11:31.490
So what you tend to find
is people within group

00:11:31.490 --> 00:11:33.660
A, B, and C will actually
have a view about how

00:11:33.660 --> 00:11:34.410
their world looks.

00:11:34.410 --> 00:11:36.450
And maybe they have
specific user stories

00:11:36.450 --> 00:11:40.410
that are giving them some niche
aspect to the design patterns.

00:11:40.410 --> 00:11:42.997
Well, what they tend to
do is build the ecosystem

00:11:42.997 --> 00:11:44.955
above and beyond their
own boundary conditions,

00:11:44.955 --> 00:11:47.160
and then hand over the
data or the information

00:11:47.160 --> 00:11:48.510
to the next group.

00:11:48.510 --> 00:11:51.360
So then you end up in data
translation and ecosystem

00:11:51.360 --> 00:11:54.000
translation, and
then reconciliations

00:11:54.000 --> 00:11:55.620
and that style of problem.

00:11:55.620 --> 00:11:57.750
So again-- and it hits--

00:11:57.750 --> 00:12:00.789
like I said, it's not just
our industry specifically.

00:12:00.789 --> 00:12:01.830
There's other industries.

00:12:01.830 --> 00:12:04.050
But I think it can be that
people then start thinking

00:12:04.050 --> 00:12:05.420
about technology choices.

00:12:05.420 --> 00:12:07.170
Rather than actually
thinking about what's

00:12:07.170 --> 00:12:12.260
optimal for their solution front
to back or from left to right,

00:12:12.260 --> 00:12:14.760
more about thinking about how
their organizational construct

00:12:14.760 --> 00:12:15.474
is driving that.

00:12:15.474 --> 00:12:16.890
So again, one of
the things that I

00:12:16.890 --> 00:12:18.723
wanted to challenge a
little bit was that we

00:12:18.723 --> 00:12:20.193
don't need to think about--

00:12:20.193 --> 00:12:21.776
when we think about
it in an evolution

00:12:21.776 --> 00:12:23.667
or a revolutionary
sense, I guess--

00:12:23.667 --> 00:12:25.500
is to think about how
we can offer something

00:12:25.500 --> 00:12:28.560
out that actually crosses those
boundaries naturally and has

00:12:28.560 --> 00:12:32.130
a very integrated stream
based and batch based approach

00:12:32.130 --> 00:12:34.710
as well to the problem.

00:12:34.710 --> 00:12:39.390
REZA ROKNI: So a little bit more
about BEAM now, and a better

00:12:39.390 --> 00:12:41.160
person than me to
describe it is Reuven,

00:12:41.160 --> 00:12:45.030
one of the technical leads
who built BEAM, and Dataflow,

00:12:45.030 --> 00:12:46.155
the [? runner ?] for BEAM.

00:12:46.155 --> 00:12:47.946
REUVEN LAX: Yeah, thank
you, Reza and Neil.

00:12:47.946 --> 00:12:49.956
Oh, thank you for the clicker.

00:12:53.110 --> 00:12:55.360
This use case is very
interesting to me,

00:12:55.360 --> 00:12:58.320
because as Reza mentioned,
I think a lot of people

00:12:58.320 --> 00:13:01.100
see Dataflow as an ETL tool.

00:13:01.100 --> 00:13:03.990
It's a very common use case to
use Dataflow to get your data

00:13:03.990 --> 00:13:06.810
into files or into BigQuery.

00:13:06.810 --> 00:13:10.260
But Dataflow is actually
a programming model,

00:13:10.260 --> 00:13:12.900
and it's a programming model
that fits this grid use

00:13:12.900 --> 00:13:14.820
case very well.

00:13:14.820 --> 00:13:19.080
You divide your logic into
massively parallel computation

00:13:19.080 --> 00:13:21.150
with its stateless functions.

00:13:23.740 --> 00:13:27.330
And then at key points,
you also put aggregations,

00:13:27.330 --> 00:13:32.100
where you need to bring the
data together to compute a count

00:13:32.100 --> 00:13:35.520
or to write it out to BigQuery.

00:13:35.520 --> 00:13:38.040
And the key to this
is, as Reza said,

00:13:38.040 --> 00:13:42.750
this data movement, which
is one of these things that

00:13:42.750 --> 00:13:43.540
sounds easy.

00:13:43.540 --> 00:13:45.120
You just have to
move data around.

00:13:45.120 --> 00:13:48.300
And it turns out that
writing a system that

00:13:48.300 --> 00:13:50.370
can move massive
amounts of data,

00:13:50.370 --> 00:13:56.190
at scale, efficiently,
with strong semantics,

00:13:56.190 --> 00:14:00.150
never moving an element twice,
never duplicating an element,

00:14:00.150 --> 00:14:02.640
is surprisingly hard to do.

00:14:02.640 --> 00:14:04.110
And integrating
that all together

00:14:04.110 --> 00:14:07.230
is one of the advantages you get
out of Dataflow that makes it

00:14:07.230 --> 00:14:11.280
work really well for
systems like this.

00:14:11.280 --> 00:14:15.720
So the way we have Dataflow
designed and presented

00:14:15.720 --> 00:14:18.980
is as a three layer system.

00:14:18.980 --> 00:14:20.680
So we have APIs.

00:14:20.680 --> 00:14:24.450
Currently, we have a Java
API and a Python API,

00:14:24.450 --> 00:14:26.230
and we have more APIs coming.

00:14:26.230 --> 00:14:29.340
So there is a Go API,
which is usable today

00:14:29.340 --> 00:14:30.380
in an early access.

00:14:30.380 --> 00:14:32.210
There's also a
SQL API, so you'll

00:14:32.210 --> 00:14:36.186
be able to write Dataflow
pipelines entirely in SQL.

00:14:36.186 --> 00:14:38.310
Then there's this programming
model I talked about.

00:14:38.310 --> 00:14:42.090
All these APIs just live on
top of this programming model,

00:14:42.090 --> 00:14:46.450
and then multiple runners
to run your pipeline.

00:14:46.450 --> 00:14:47.940
So there's the
Google hosted runner

00:14:47.940 --> 00:14:49.180
called Google Cloud Dataflow.

00:14:49.180 --> 00:14:50.670
This is this fully
managed runner.

00:14:50.670 --> 00:14:53.450
We just point it at
our cloud and it runs.

00:14:53.450 --> 00:14:57.120
We also have open-source
runners, one on top of a system

00:14:57.120 --> 00:14:58.920
called Apache Flink,
one on top of a system

00:14:58.920 --> 00:15:00.780
called Apache Spark.

00:15:00.780 --> 00:15:05.340
So your Dataflow
pipelines are not

00:15:05.340 --> 00:15:07.800
bound to only
running on Dataflow.

00:15:07.800 --> 00:15:10.080
So this is interesting
in the hybrid cloud case.

00:15:10.080 --> 00:15:13.454
We've also found in the
financial services industry,

00:15:13.454 --> 00:15:15.120
this has been interesting
to some people

00:15:15.120 --> 00:15:16.590
for regulatory reasons.

00:15:16.590 --> 00:15:20.220
If a job needs to
be rerun on prem,

00:15:20.220 --> 00:15:22.260
if a regulator
requests it, it can

00:15:22.260 --> 00:15:24.180
be done with one of these
open source runners.

00:15:27.140 --> 00:15:31.400
So what is the advantage of
the Cloud Dataflow system?

00:15:31.400 --> 00:15:36.120
So Cloud Dataflow is one of
the runners for this BEAM API.

00:15:36.120 --> 00:15:41.240
So Cloud Dataflow just presents
this fully managed, no-op

00:15:41.240 --> 00:15:43.320
serverless solution.

00:15:43.320 --> 00:15:46.000
So you don't have to--

00:15:46.000 --> 00:15:51.170
the SDK gives you a nice, simple
API to develop these things.

00:15:51.170 --> 00:15:53.990
But then Dataflow
manages security for you.

00:15:53.990 --> 00:15:55.800
It manages the cluster for you.

00:15:55.800 --> 00:16:00.690
You don't have to spin up a
cluster to run your pipelines.

00:16:00.690 --> 00:16:02.760
It will auto-scale
your pipelines.

00:16:02.760 --> 00:16:06.290
So Dataflow will attempt to
learn how big of a cluster

00:16:06.290 --> 00:16:09.500
you need to run your
pipeline, and it will actually

00:16:09.500 --> 00:16:12.800
shrink and grow this
cluster over time.

00:16:12.800 --> 00:16:14.720
As you get towards
the end of your job

00:16:14.720 --> 00:16:16.310
and there's less
data left, Dataflow

00:16:16.310 --> 00:16:17.851
will just start
shrinking the cluster

00:16:17.851 --> 00:16:19.970
and try to optimize cost
as much as possible.

00:16:23.820 --> 00:16:28.090
There's also a lot of
integration with TensorFlow

00:16:28.090 --> 00:16:30.300
based machine learning models.

00:16:30.300 --> 00:16:33.020
So there are TensorFlow
APIs that you can integrate

00:16:33.020 --> 00:16:40.190
inside your Dataflow job, and
there's a suite of TensorFlow

00:16:40.190 --> 00:16:44.060
based transforms that
you can run via Dataflow.

00:16:44.060 --> 00:16:46.310
So there's a suite
called TFX which

00:16:46.310 --> 00:16:49.850
is a suite of machine
learning transforms

00:16:49.850 --> 00:16:55.550
for prepping and evaluating
machine learning models.

00:16:55.550 --> 00:16:59.720
So a classic
example of that is I

00:16:59.720 --> 00:17:01.514
trained a new machine
learning model,

00:17:01.514 --> 00:17:02.930
and I run a simple
analysis on it.

00:17:02.930 --> 00:17:06.770
It says, OK, it's 5%
better than my last model.

00:17:06.770 --> 00:17:08.329
Then you deploy it,
and you find out

00:17:08.329 --> 00:17:11.089
that, oh, it was 5%
better on average,

00:17:11.089 --> 00:17:15.260
but it was producing
worse results

00:17:15.260 --> 00:17:18.619
for everybody from the state
of Florida, for instance.

00:17:18.619 --> 00:17:20.240
This is actually
not a better model.

00:17:20.240 --> 00:17:23.329
So it turns out
evaluating these models is

00:17:23.329 --> 00:17:27.280
fairly tricky to make sure that
the model actually is better.

00:17:27.280 --> 00:17:30.140
TFX provides suites
of tools to do things.

00:17:30.140 --> 00:17:32.360
That's one example of the
tools that TFX provide,

00:17:32.360 --> 00:17:33.830
is good evaluation of models.

00:17:33.830 --> 00:17:36.750
And this all runs on
top of Cloud Dataflow.

00:17:41.820 --> 00:17:46.810
So I mentioned that Dataflow
lets you run serverless data

00:17:46.810 --> 00:17:49.007
analytics, as opposed
to other clusters

00:17:49.007 --> 00:17:50.340
where you have to run a cluster.

00:17:50.340 --> 00:17:54.390
So what is the benefit of
serverless data analytics?

00:17:54.390 --> 00:17:57.510
Well, if you look, this
little chart on the left

00:17:57.510 --> 00:17:59.580
is traditionally what
you would have to do

00:17:59.580 --> 00:18:02.080
to run your big data analytics.

00:18:02.080 --> 00:18:04.500
So there's a tiny little
piece of your work

00:18:04.500 --> 00:18:07.440
in which you actually
worked on your analysis

00:18:07.440 --> 00:18:10.980
and your insights, your
business logic, your functions,

00:18:10.980 --> 00:18:14.640
the actual quant stuff that
Neil wants his people to spend

00:18:14.640 --> 00:18:17.010
most of their time working on.

00:18:17.010 --> 00:18:19.350
And then you would
actually spend about 90%

00:18:19.350 --> 00:18:23.100
of your time on everything else,
building a monitoring solution

00:18:23.100 --> 00:18:27.240
to make sure your job is
running, performance tuning,

00:18:27.240 --> 00:18:30.450
figuring out how many workers
do I need to run this on?

00:18:30.450 --> 00:18:33.420
How much memory should
I give each worker?

00:18:33.420 --> 00:18:35.624
What particular type
of worker should I be?

00:18:35.624 --> 00:18:37.040
Should I run on
four core workers?

00:18:37.040 --> 00:18:38.415
Should I run on
two core workers?

00:18:38.415 --> 00:18:41.570
Should I run on 16 core workers?

00:18:41.570 --> 00:18:47.190
Then making sure my
utilization is up to snuff.

00:18:47.190 --> 00:18:50.351
Then figuring out a deployment
story and a configuration

00:18:50.351 --> 00:18:50.850
story.

00:18:50.850 --> 00:18:54.192
And people would often
spend a huge amount of time

00:18:54.192 --> 00:18:55.650
coming up with
different deployment

00:18:55.650 --> 00:18:58.320
stories for these systems.

00:18:58.320 --> 00:19:02.350
Resource provisioning,
handling growing scale.

00:19:02.350 --> 00:19:04.217
So I came up with
something that worked,

00:19:04.217 --> 00:19:06.300
and then suddenly, six
months later, my input data

00:19:06.300 --> 00:19:07.916
is 50% larger.

00:19:07.916 --> 00:19:09.915
The thing I came up with
before no longer works.

00:19:12.402 --> 00:19:13.860
Resetting, going
back to the start,

00:19:13.860 --> 00:19:16.230
and running through this
whole process again.

00:19:16.230 --> 00:19:18.240
Reliability,
setting up alerting,

00:19:18.240 --> 00:19:22.100
making sure that these
jobs actually run reliably

00:19:22.100 --> 00:19:24.800
and complete, as I expect.

00:19:24.800 --> 00:19:29.010
The advantage of serverless
is to cut out all of this pie

00:19:29.010 --> 00:19:31.650
except for the
analysis and insights.

00:19:31.650 --> 00:19:34.350
Focus on the actual
business logic

00:19:34.350 --> 00:19:36.360
you want to run
in your pipeline,

00:19:36.360 --> 00:19:38.760
and let the serverless
system, in this case,

00:19:38.760 --> 00:19:41.510
Google Cloud Dataflow,
handle all the rest for you.

00:19:44.670 --> 00:19:48.270
And finally, we've been
saying that Dataflow not just

00:19:48.270 --> 00:19:49.100
a product.

00:19:49.100 --> 00:19:50.370
It's a platform.

00:19:50.370 --> 00:19:52.800
And Google Cloud
platform is the platform

00:19:52.800 --> 00:19:54.960
that Dataflow is part of.

00:19:54.960 --> 00:19:58.150
So a platform is actually
not just one technology.

00:19:58.150 --> 00:20:02.540
It's a group of
technologies that

00:20:02.540 --> 00:20:08.520
often act as a substrate to
build higher level systems.

00:20:08.520 --> 00:20:11.900
So as Reza's going
to show you here,

00:20:11.900 --> 00:20:14.240
an interesting thing
here for this problem

00:20:14.240 --> 00:20:16.640
is not just Dataflow,
but how can I

00:20:16.640 --> 00:20:21.680
use Dataflow in conjunction
with BigQuery and in conjunction

00:20:21.680 --> 00:20:27.260
with Google Cloud Storage, and
use all these things together

00:20:27.260 --> 00:20:29.700
to provide a solution?

00:20:29.700 --> 00:20:32.500
And since Dataflow flow
has sources and sinks--

00:20:32.500 --> 00:20:33.750
as we're going to show here--

00:20:33.750 --> 00:20:38.510
Dataflow has sources and sinks
to all these other GCP stores

00:20:38.510 --> 00:20:43.340
and data resources, Dataflow
provides a great substrate

00:20:43.340 --> 00:20:45.530
to link all of these
things together.

00:20:45.530 --> 00:20:48.470
So with Dataflow-- so you
have sources and sinks.

00:20:48.470 --> 00:20:50.360
You have an API,
which, in this case,

00:20:50.360 --> 00:20:54.260
is the Dataflow API, which is
your way of linking everything

00:20:54.260 --> 00:20:59.810
together, saying
read from this data,

00:20:59.810 --> 00:21:02.510
run these transforms over
it, write to BigQuery,

00:21:02.510 --> 00:21:05.600
write to Bigtable, write to
Spanner, write to Pub/Sub,

00:21:05.600 --> 00:21:08.830
write to whatever
other sink you want to.

00:21:08.830 --> 00:21:10.820
And your pipeline
is declarative.

00:21:10.820 --> 00:21:16.687
And this image here is actually
an example of a Dataflow graph,

00:21:16.687 --> 00:21:18.770
in fact, I believe the one
that Reza will show you

00:21:18.770 --> 00:21:23.320
in a second of running data.

00:21:23.320 --> 00:21:24.370
And now Reza?

00:21:24.370 --> 00:21:25.579
REZA ROKNI: Yeah, so--

00:21:25.579 --> 00:21:26.620
if I can have the ticket.

00:21:26.620 --> 00:21:26.870
REUVEN LAX: Oh yeah, sorry.

00:21:26.870 --> 00:21:28.190
REZA ROKNI: Thank you.

00:21:28.190 --> 00:21:30.680
So just a couple of things
around the other bits

00:21:30.680 --> 00:21:31.940
of technology we use.

00:21:31.940 --> 00:21:34.250
I, when I was doing the
experimentation with Neil,

00:21:34.250 --> 00:21:38.100
I don't actually have access
to UBS Bank's data, obviously.

00:21:38.100 --> 00:21:39.800
I have to work in
my own environment.

00:21:39.800 --> 00:21:41.840
And we wanted to make
this as real as possible.

00:21:41.840 --> 00:21:44.870
I didn't just want to make
synthetic data, especially

00:21:44.870 --> 00:21:47.270
for the inputs and
the ETL layers.

00:21:47.270 --> 00:21:48.830
So what we were
using was BigQuery.

00:21:48.830 --> 00:21:51.800
Now BigQuery is Google's
petabyte scale data

00:21:51.800 --> 00:21:55.340
warehouse in the cloud,
fully managed and serverless.

00:21:55.340 --> 00:21:58.100
One of the key aspects that
I was making use of here

00:21:58.100 --> 00:22:00.980
is that it separates
processing from storage,

00:22:00.980 --> 00:22:04.370
which means sharing of
data is very, very easy.

00:22:04.370 --> 00:22:05.990
And this meant I
could get access

00:22:05.990 --> 00:22:07.700
to external data sources.

00:22:07.700 --> 00:22:10.520
In particular, Thomson
Reuters were very kind

00:22:10.520 --> 00:22:12.630
enough to provide me data.

00:22:12.630 --> 00:22:16.490
So they have a store
in their project

00:22:16.490 --> 00:22:18.860
where they're putting
historic tick information

00:22:18.860 --> 00:22:21.530
and current tick information,
including things like swaps,

00:22:21.530 --> 00:22:24.320
deposits, et cetera, which I
need to use to build things

00:22:24.320 --> 00:22:25.970
like yield curves.

00:22:25.970 --> 00:22:28.790
They also, four
POCs, they can put up

00:22:28.790 --> 00:22:33.030
to 20 years of historical
data on to BigQuery.

00:22:33.030 --> 00:22:35.859
And I get the benefit
of just instantly being

00:22:35.859 --> 00:22:37.400
able to use that
stuff without having

00:22:37.400 --> 00:22:38.690
to move it around projects.

00:22:38.690 --> 00:22:40.760
So again, thank you,
Thomson Reuters,

00:22:40.760 --> 00:22:42.710
for helping me do this.

00:22:42.710 --> 00:22:46.520
If I can-- one of
the other pieces

00:22:46.520 --> 00:22:48.500
that we won't show you
in the demo right now,

00:22:48.500 --> 00:22:51.920
but we will be using in
the real thing, is today

00:22:51.920 --> 00:22:55.040
I'm actually sending even
the raw data to BigQuery,

00:22:55.040 --> 00:22:58.724
like very granular data, just
so I can show it to you guys.

00:22:58.724 --> 00:23:00.140
But in the real
production system,

00:23:00.140 --> 00:23:02.630
we'd send outputs,
the results, which

00:23:02.630 --> 00:23:05.900
is still many billions of rows
of information, to BigQuery.

00:23:05.900 --> 00:23:08.510
But the raw data
that we want to keep

00:23:08.510 --> 00:23:10.070
is going to be many,
many terabytes.

00:23:10.070 --> 00:23:11.990
That, I would send to Bigtable.

00:23:11.990 --> 00:23:15.110
Bigtable linearly scales,
and we use it internally

00:23:15.110 --> 00:23:18.027
within Google for solving
exactly that kind of problem.

00:23:18.027 --> 00:23:19.610
And it's perfect for
this environment,

00:23:19.610 --> 00:23:22.490
because we have many
thousands of cores,

00:23:22.490 --> 00:23:24.620
all running, generating data.

00:23:24.620 --> 00:23:26.780
And if we don't want
to create a bottleneck,

00:23:26.780 --> 00:23:29.720
we can just use Bigtable
and linearly scale it out,

00:23:29.720 --> 00:23:32.060
and have all of those
cores just push data

00:23:32.060 --> 00:23:35.810
directly into Bigtable at
many, many tens of thousands

00:23:35.810 --> 00:23:39.830
or hundreds-- actually,
hundreds of thousands of QPS.

00:23:39.830 --> 00:23:42.830
So with that, Neil
is going to walk

00:23:42.830 --> 00:23:46.760
through what the
pipeline's actually doing

00:23:46.760 --> 00:23:49.340
in terms of the finance side.

00:23:49.340 --> 00:23:53.350
NEIL BOSTON: Yeah, so I guess
based on the 3.1 wishes,

00:23:53.350 --> 00:23:56.300
I set up a very
basic experiment.

00:23:56.300 --> 00:23:58.430
I mean, I don't know how
much background you have,

00:23:58.430 --> 00:24:01.430
I guess, in investment
banking and markets piece,

00:24:01.430 --> 00:24:05.480
but basically, the most basic
experiment I could think of

00:24:05.480 --> 00:24:08.380
was the source market
environments or market data.

00:24:08.380 --> 00:24:10.820
So that could be things
like FX spot or yield curves

00:24:10.820 --> 00:24:13.130
of volatilities.

00:24:13.130 --> 00:24:15.620
Take a portfolio,
a set of trades.

00:24:15.620 --> 00:24:17.990
In this case, we
just randomly created

00:24:17.990 --> 00:24:21.440
a million option trades.

00:24:21.440 --> 00:24:23.310
Basically, take that
as our underlying data.

00:24:23.310 --> 00:24:25.340
Process it, as you can see here.

00:24:25.340 --> 00:24:29.370
Apply some transforms to the
market data, which is really--

00:24:29.370 --> 00:24:32.010
you can select a
set of market data.

00:24:32.010 --> 00:24:33.950
You then have to apply--

00:24:33.950 --> 00:24:37.640
in this case, we used an
open source quant library

00:24:37.640 --> 00:24:40.070
to produce a yield
curve functor, I guess,

00:24:40.070 --> 00:24:43.280
at the end of the day, an
object, from the input.

00:24:43.280 --> 00:24:44.144
Process the data.

00:24:44.144 --> 00:24:45.560
I also wanted to
create scenarios.

00:24:45.560 --> 00:24:47.810
So what I mean by
that is in the market,

00:24:47.810 --> 00:24:49.650
you tend to have a set
of points coming in.

00:24:49.650 --> 00:24:52.130
You also want to
shift things around.

00:24:52.130 --> 00:24:54.770
So you could end up with a
curve with 10 points on it,

00:24:54.770 --> 00:24:55.520
for example.

00:24:55.520 --> 00:24:58.684
And I want to shift each
point independently,

00:24:58.684 --> 00:25:00.850
and I also want to do these
parallel shifts as well.

00:25:00.850 --> 00:25:03.850
So you can see I wanted to
try and create scenarios

00:25:03.850 --> 00:25:05.920
on the fly which I
thought would be good just

00:25:05.920 --> 00:25:08.200
from an experimentation
point of view.

00:25:08.200 --> 00:25:12.340
Then I wanted to run a set of
analytics on it, some of which

00:25:12.340 --> 00:25:15.440
I coded in, which is very basic.

00:25:15.440 --> 00:25:17.630
And then analyze the experiment.

00:25:17.630 --> 00:25:20.122
So this was kind of the
most basic experiment

00:25:20.122 --> 00:25:20.830
I could think of.

00:25:20.830 --> 00:25:24.840
I could choose a different trade
set to make it easier to price

00:25:24.840 --> 00:25:26.940
and easier to risk.

00:25:26.940 --> 00:25:29.760
But again, it was just
personal bias and preference

00:25:29.760 --> 00:25:33.610
that I wanted to try options
from a personal perspective.

00:25:33.610 --> 00:25:36.450
So based on the 3.1 wishes, this
was the most basic experiment

00:25:36.450 --> 00:25:38.116
that I thought we
could start with, just

00:25:38.116 --> 00:25:39.700
to see what that looked like.

00:25:39.700 --> 00:25:42.880
Scenarios, again, I just chose
very market standard things,

00:25:42.880 --> 00:25:45.750
non-parallel shifts, weighted
shifts, independent shifts,

00:25:45.750 --> 00:25:48.760
and parallel shifts, again,
to create a much larger set

00:25:48.760 --> 00:25:52.024
of output datasets, more in line
with what I see in the market.

00:25:52.024 --> 00:25:52.940
REZA ROKNI: Thank you.

