00:29:13.997 --> 00:29:16.330
One thing that we did as an
experiment that's worked out

00:29:16.330 --> 00:29:21.130
quite well is rather
than have the trade flow

00:29:21.130 --> 00:29:25.390
through the DAG with
introspection to the data

00:29:25.390 --> 00:29:29.300
sources for all the data that
they need, I reversed this.

00:29:29.300 --> 00:29:32.590
So what we have is all of
the trades come as a lump,

00:29:32.590 --> 00:29:34.120
as a side input.

00:29:34.120 --> 00:29:36.130
I do all the ETL and
processing that I

00:29:36.130 --> 00:29:39.380
need to do, including scenario
generation, from the top.

00:29:39.380 --> 00:29:42.100
And then what I do is, because
the introspection would have

00:29:42.100 --> 00:29:44.650
been lots of long RPC calls--

00:29:44.650 --> 00:29:48.360
not long, fast-- but RPC calls,
which are still expensive,

00:29:48.360 --> 00:29:51.160
what I do by bringing the trades
and all the scenarios together,

00:29:51.160 --> 00:29:54.350
I just do a really dumb
massive fan-out with a loop.

00:29:54.350 --> 00:29:56.500
So what it does, it just
generates huge amounts

00:29:56.500 --> 00:29:58.709
of data tuples.

00:29:58.709 --> 00:30:00.250
So the data that I
need to run, which

00:30:00.250 --> 00:30:02.950
includes all of the values
from the yield curves,

00:30:02.950 --> 00:30:05.740
for example, which is an array
of two and half thousand,

00:30:05.740 --> 00:30:07.370
in this case, doubles--

00:30:07.370 --> 00:30:09.100
I generate all these
values and just let

00:30:09.100 --> 00:30:10.930
Dataflow do what
it's good at, which

00:30:10.930 --> 00:30:14.530
is move lots of data around for
me and distribute that work.

00:30:14.530 --> 00:30:16.240
So here we have
the trades that are

00:30:16.240 --> 00:30:19.000
being read coming
in from the side

00:30:19.000 --> 00:30:20.889
and being added in
as a side input.

00:30:20.889 --> 00:30:22.930
So if we wanted just to
explain a little bit more

00:30:22.930 --> 00:30:24.819
on what side inputs are--

00:30:24.819 --> 00:30:26.860
so if you think of kind
of like a broadcast join,

00:30:26.860 --> 00:30:29.500
if your data is of
a certain size that

00:30:29.500 --> 00:30:33.494
will fit in the environment,
rather than do a shuffle join

00:30:33.494 --> 00:30:35.410
where we're joining all
the elements together,

00:30:35.410 --> 00:30:37.330
you just take this-- it's
like having a smaller table.

00:30:37.330 --> 00:30:38.800
We take that smaller
table and just

00:30:38.800 --> 00:30:41.260
make it available to
all the environments.

00:30:41.260 --> 00:30:43.330
And then when my
scenarios are coming in,

00:30:43.330 --> 00:30:45.250
because all that data's there,
they're just looping through.

00:30:45.250 --> 00:30:47.680
And any trades that happen to
match the currency that I'm

00:30:47.680 --> 00:30:51.950
looking for come out as a
fan-out of the scenarios.

00:30:51.950 --> 00:30:57.490
So here we have the
scenarios being generated.

00:30:57.490 --> 00:30:59.650
I actually do a little
bit of optimization,

00:30:59.650 --> 00:31:03.370
in that for each piece
of work, we actually

00:31:03.370 --> 00:31:06.880
make it 100 scenarios that
needs to be processed,

00:31:06.880 --> 00:31:09.490
because it's a bit more
efficient in the next stage.

00:31:09.490 --> 00:31:13.540
Here we have-- this
is Neil's code.

00:31:13.540 --> 00:31:16.420
So this is C++ code
running in Dataflow.

00:31:16.420 --> 00:31:17.890
Neil has written a--

00:31:17.890 --> 00:31:20.186
I'll let him describe it,
because I have no idea.

00:31:20.186 --> 00:31:22.949
NEIL BOSTON: Well, I guess
basically, I took the--

00:31:22.949 --> 00:31:25.240
I don't know how familiar
you are with options pricing.

00:31:25.240 --> 00:31:28.280
There's nothing
particularly smart about it.

00:31:28.280 --> 00:31:30.400
Black and Scholes
wrote a paper in 1973

00:31:30.400 --> 00:31:31.845
on how to price options.

00:31:31.845 --> 00:31:33.970
They managed to show that
it was like the diffusion

00:31:33.970 --> 00:31:36.640
equation, which is like a
partial differential equation.

00:31:36.640 --> 00:31:38.140
So all I did is
write a thing called

00:31:38.140 --> 00:31:40.960
finite difference for a way of
discretizing and solving that.

00:31:40.960 --> 00:31:44.200
But ultimately, all I'm really
doing in a C++ is doing matrix,

00:31:44.200 --> 00:31:47.510
sparse matrix, solving
calculations over time.

00:31:47.510 --> 00:31:50.590
So it's nothing particularly
smart if you look at the code.

00:31:50.590 --> 00:31:54.130
It's probably the least smart
piece of the whole ecosystem,

00:31:54.130 --> 00:31:54.660
I guess.

00:31:54.660 --> 00:31:55.870
REZA ROKNI: Well,
that's what he thinks.

00:31:55.870 --> 00:31:56.822
I tried to read it.

00:31:56.822 --> 00:31:58.946
NEIL BOSTON: I also took
16 days as well to run it.

00:31:58.946 --> 00:32:01.455
These were the biggest
out of all that as well.

00:32:01.455 --> 00:32:04.040
REZA ROKNI: Well, that's where
the real work happens here,

00:32:04.040 --> 00:32:04.540
right.

00:32:04.540 --> 00:32:05.540
And this was a good--

00:32:05.540 --> 00:32:07.630
we use that function
as something

00:32:07.630 --> 00:32:10.150
as a good indicator of what
a real thing would look like,

00:32:10.150 --> 00:32:11.944
because it's doing real work.

00:32:11.944 --> 00:32:13.360
And this is where
most of the work

00:32:13.360 --> 00:32:14.776
is happening for
this environment.

00:32:14.776 --> 00:32:17.140
So up until now,
we've been doing ETL.

00:32:17.140 --> 00:32:18.847
We've been setting
up all the scenarios.

00:32:18.847 --> 00:32:20.680
I've been doing these
