00:32:42.350 --> 00:32:46.540
What we do is, the code
that Neil has written,

00:32:46.540 --> 00:32:48.340
he's got a spot index,
which is actually

00:32:48.340 --> 00:32:49.870
the thing we really want.

00:32:49.870 --> 00:32:53.736
But he also has an array of
information, which is the--

00:32:53.736 --> 00:32:55.360
NEIL BOSTON: Yeah,
so basically, when I

00:32:55.360 --> 00:32:57.850
do the matrix calculation,
which iterate over time

00:32:57.850 --> 00:33:00.730
to some answer, you get
basically a vector back,

00:33:00.730 --> 00:33:02.580
based against
different spot levels.

00:33:02.580 --> 00:33:04.330
But I actually want
the one in the middle,

00:33:04.330 --> 00:33:05.950
because that's the
current spot level.

00:33:05.950 --> 00:33:06.990
REZA ROKNI: So that's
the thing we wanted,

00:33:06.990 --> 00:33:08.989
and that's the thing we
should send to BigQuery.

00:33:08.989 --> 00:33:10.249
However, we want to reco--

00:33:10.249 --> 00:33:12.790
one of the advantages of being
able to use this data platform

00:33:12.790 --> 00:33:14.920
is at each stage
of the pipeline,

00:33:14.920 --> 00:33:18.222
I can output results and
go back and look at them.

00:33:18.222 --> 00:33:19.930
Because I don't care
about the IO, right.

00:33:19.930 --> 00:33:22.250
It's just, Bigtable
will deal with it.

00:33:22.250 --> 00:33:24.580
So what we do here
is, I'm actually

00:33:24.580 --> 00:33:26.739
taking all of those
values as well

00:33:26.739 --> 00:33:28.030
and dumping them into BigQuery.

00:33:28.030 --> 00:33:30.610
Now I did that so you guys
