00:40:49.690 --> 00:40:52.330
that we could break these
problems apart from what we've

00:40:52.330 --> 00:40:54.520
seen in the past,
just as someone

00:40:54.520 --> 00:40:56.794
writing some quantitative code.

00:40:56.794 --> 00:40:58.210
REUVEN LAX: This
is a common thing

00:40:58.210 --> 00:41:02.800
we've seen as people move
from their old systems

00:41:02.800 --> 00:41:04.960
to new systems, is
people have code

00:41:04.960 --> 00:41:08.260
that was written assuming
I'm running on one machine.

00:41:08.260 --> 00:41:10.930
And it would be highly
optimized in ways

00:41:10.930 --> 00:41:14.750
that make it very difficult to
parallelize, like oh, sometimes

00:41:14.750 --> 00:41:17.050
I'll spin up threads and
then join on these threads,

00:41:17.050 --> 00:41:22.090
or interleave many
different functions

00:41:22.090 --> 00:41:23.810
throughout my calculation.

00:41:23.810 --> 00:41:25.810
So it sometimes requires
people to rethink

00:41:25.810 --> 00:41:30.055
how they wrote the code, or
maybe on a mathematical sense,

00:41:30.055 --> 00:41:32.180
look at your functions,
say, can these functions be

00:41:32.180 --> 00:41:35.830
[? linearalized ?]
as a combination

00:41:35.830 --> 00:41:38.200
of multiple other
functions, so that we

00:41:38.200 --> 00:41:39.970
can parallelize all
these things out

00:41:39.970 --> 00:41:43.280
and get a finer result later?

00:41:43.280 --> 00:41:46.280
REZA ROKNI: And so what
that means is that when

00:41:46.280 --> 00:41:50.720
