00:42:22.490 --> 00:42:27.250
It's not my area, and
neither is the finance area.

00:42:27.250 --> 00:42:29.570
However, when we break
the problem down,

00:42:29.570 --> 00:42:32.180
for me as that
engineer, to bytes--

00:42:32.180 --> 00:42:36.230
so he can tell me, I need
these bytes to move from here,

00:42:36.230 --> 00:42:38.660
and I need to run this
function against these bytes.

00:42:38.660 --> 00:42:40.370
This output needs to move here.

00:42:40.370 --> 00:42:42.440
When you start breaking
this down into a way

00:42:42.440 --> 00:42:45.260
that I understand, it
becomes very easy for us

00:42:45.260 --> 00:42:46.640
to start communicating.

00:42:46.640 --> 00:42:49.250
And one of the things that
we used as a common language

00:42:49.250 --> 00:42:51.110
is protobufs.

00:42:51.110 --> 00:42:53.770
So we started talking
in terms-- so protobufs,

00:42:53.770 --> 00:42:56.500
I'll let Reuven do
what protobufs are.

00:42:56.500 --> 00:42:59.340
REUVEN LAX: Yeah,
a protocol buffer,

00:42:59.340 --> 00:43:02.080
it's a common structured
format for data

00:43:02.080 --> 00:43:06.290
that has been used at Google
since probably around 2001,

00:43:06.290 --> 00:43:07.110
2002.

00:43:07.110 --> 00:43:09.720
So it's been used at
Google for quite a while.

00:43:09.720 --> 00:43:14.930
It's very similar to other
forms of structured data.

00:43:14.930 --> 00:43:16.760
So some people use Avro.

00:43:16.760 --> 00:43:18.560
Some people use Kryo.

00:43:18.560 --> 00:43:19.702
Some people use Thrift.

00:43:19.702 --> 00:43:21.410
There are other forms
of structured data.

00:43:21.410 --> 00:43:24.230
Protobufs are an optimized one
that Google has traditionally

00:43:24.230 --> 00:43:27.447
used and is also open sourced.

00:43:27.447 --> 00:43:29.030
REZA ROKNI: And that
made it very easy

00:43:29.030 --> 00:43:30.446
for me to start
working with Neil,

00:43:30.446 --> 00:43:33.770
because I could just give
him a definition of how I'm

00:43:33.770 --> 00:43:35.420
going to pass things
through, or he

00:43:35.420 --> 00:43:36.770
could give me the definition.

00:43:36.770 --> 00:43:38.150
And then I know
what I need to do

00:43:38.150 --> 00:43:39.770
in terms of doing this process.

00:43:39.770 --> 00:43:42.560
And the other thing it
opens up is this ability

00:43:42.560 --> 00:43:44.360
now to actually break
apart the functions.

00:43:44.360 --> 00:43:48.350
Because if all you're passing
as the parameter to the function

00:43:48.350 --> 00:43:51.230
is a protobuf, and
within your code,

00:43:51.230 --> 00:43:54.260
you start just using protobufs
to pass the information around,

00:43:54.260 --> 00:43:57.920
when you break apart
the big pieces of code,

00:43:57.920 --> 00:44:00.560
it becomes very easy to
plug it into the DAG,

00:44:00.560 --> 00:44:02.780
because all I'm doing
is sending protobufs

00:44:02.780 --> 00:44:07.190
across those nodes and that data
movement that we talked about.

00:44:07.190 --> 00:44:09.770
So with that, next
steps, as we said,

